<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kost Levytsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kostiantyn Levytsky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kost Levytsky was a Ukrainian lawyer and politician in Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, a leader of the National <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-united-states">Democratic Party</a> and member of the Austrian parliament. In November 1918 he headed the first government of the West Ukrainian People's Republic, the State Secretariat, under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yevhen-petrushevych">Yevhen Petrushevych</a>. In 1941 he briefly chaired the Ukrainian National Council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> before his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kost-levytsky.jpg" alt="Kost Levytsky" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown-anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Levytsky_Kost-1-.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kost Antonovych Levytsky was born on 18 November 1859 at Tysmenytsia, the eldest child of the Greek Catholic priest Antin Levytsky. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivano-frankivsk">Stanislaviv</a> he studied law at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, took his doctorate in 1884 and opened a barrister's office in Lviv in 1890. He helped found the economic associations Zorya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> and the Province Credit Union, translated foreign laws into Ukrainian, published a German-Ukrainian law dictionary and edited the journals Chasopys pravnycha and Zhyttia i pravo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Levytsky led Narodna Rada, the People's Council of 1885, and co-founded the Ukrainian National Democratic Party. He was elected to the Austrian parliament in 1907 and to the Galician Sejm in 1908, and argued for autonomy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> as a first step towards statehood. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began he headed the Supreme Ukrainian Council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, which named Tsarist Russia the main enemy. In 1916, as a prosecutor for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>, he played a part in the death sentences passed on Ukrainian Russophiles and sent others to imprisonment at Talerhof.</p><p>After resigning as head of government in December 1918 he worked on electoral reform and foreign affairs and led missions of the West Ukrainian republic to Riga in 1920 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in 1921. When the government dissolved itself in 1923 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a>, where he sat on the central committee of the Ukrainian National Democratic Association from 1925 to 1939, directed Centrobank and wrote a history of the liberation struggles of the Galician Ukrainians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the Soviet invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in September 1939 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">People's Commissariat for Internal Affairs</a> arrested him and held him in the Lubyanka prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a>, Vyacheslav Molotov and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavrentiy-beria">Lavrentiy Beria</a> were involved in his case. He was released in the spring of 1941 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a>. As head of the Council of Seniors after the proclamation of 30 June 1941, he negotiated with the administration of Distrikt Galizien, petitioned against repressions and pleaded for prisoners, often with success. He died on 12 November 1941 and was buried at Yaniv Cemetery in Lviv.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kost Levytsky” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yevhen Petrushevych became its president and <b>Kost Levytsky</b> headed the government.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-ukrainian-peoples-republic">West Ukrainian People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tysmenytsia, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.9,24.85&amp;year=1912" aria-label="Tysmenytsia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5124 457 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kost Levytsky"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5574 727h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5574 727h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.9,24.85&amp;year=1912">What was here in 1912? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
